.TH ZIG_INTRODUCTION 7 "2026-10-06" "Zig 0.17.0" "Zig Manual"
.SH NAME
zig-introduction \- overview of the Zig programming language
.SH DESCRIPTION
Zig is a general-purpose programming language and toolchain for
maintaining robust, optimal, and reusable software.
.PP
The language is designed around four properties:
.TP
\fBRobust\fR
Behavior is correct even for edge cases such as out of memory.
.TP
\fBOptimal\fR
Write programs the best way they can behave and perform.
.TP
\fBReusable\fR
The same code works in many environments which have different
constraints.
.TP
\fBMaintainable\fR
Precisely communicate intent to the compiler and other programmers. The
language imposes a low overhead to reading code and is resilient to
changing requirements and environments.
.SH DESIGN PRINCIPLES
.SS Small, simple language
Focus on debugging your application rather than debugging your
programming language knowledge. Zig's entire syntax is specified with a
580-line PEG grammar file.
.PP
There is no hidden control flow, no hidden memory allocations, no
preprocessor, and no macros. If Zig code does not look like it is
jumping away to call a function, then it is not.
.PP
Examples of hidden control flow in other languages:
.IP \(bu 2
D has \fB@property\fR functions, which are methods called with what looks
like field access, so an expression like \fBc.d\fR might call a
function.
.IP \(bu 2
C++, D, and Rust have operator overloading, so the \fB+\fR operator
might call a function.
.IP \(bu 2
C++, D, and Go have throw/catch exceptions, so \fBfoo()\fR might throw
an exception and prevent \fBbar()\fR from being called.
.PP
Zig promotes code maintenance and readability by making all control flow
managed exclusively with language keywords and function calls.
.SS Performance and safety: choose two
Zig has four build modes, and they can all be mixed and matched all the
way down to scope granularity:
.nf
    Debug          optimize off, safety on
    ReleaseSafe    optimize on,  safety on
    ReleaseFast    optimize on,  safety off
    ReleaseSmall   optimize on,  safety off, size-focused
.fi
.PP
With Zig one can rely on a safety-enabled build mode, and selectively
disable safety at performance bottlenecks.
.PP
Zig uses Illegal Behavior as a tool for both bug prevention and
performance enhancement. In Zig both signed and unsigned integers have
illegal behavior on overflow, contrasted to only signed integers in C.
This facilitates optimizations that are not available in C.
.PP
Zig is faster than C for several reasons:
.IP \(bu 2
All Zig code lives in one compilation unit, optimized together.
.IP \(bu 2
Carefully chosen illegal behavior.
.IP \(bu 2
Zig directly exposes a SIMD vector type.
.IP \(bu 2
The standard library provides essential data structures such as hash
maps and array lists.
.IP \(bu 2
Advanced CPU features are enabled by default, unless cross-compiling.
.SS Zig competes with C instead of depending on it
The Zig Standard Library integrates with libc, but does not depend on
it. A Hello World compiled with \fB-O ReleaseSmall\fR, debug symbols
stripped, single-threaded mode, produces a 9.8 KiB static executable for
the \fBx86_64-linux\fR target. A Windows build is even smaller, coming
out to 4096 bytes.
.SS Order-independent top level declarations
Top level declarations such as global variables are order-independent
and lazily analyzed. The initialization values of global variables are
evaluated at compile-time.
.SS Optional type instead of null pointers
In other programming languages, null references are the source of many
runtime exceptions. Unadorned Zig pointers cannot be null. However, any
type can be made into an optional type by prefixing it with \fB?\fR.
.PP
To unwrap an optional value, one can use \fBorelse\fR to provide a
default value, or use \fBif\fR. The same syntax works with \fBwhile\fR.
.SS Manual memory management
A library written in Zig is eligible to be used anywhere: desktop
applications, low-latency servers, databases, operating system kernels,
embedded devices, real-time software, web browsers or other plugins with
WebAssembly, and by other programming languages using the C ABI.
.PP
Zig programmers must manage their own memory and must handle memory
allocation failure. The Zig Standard Library follows the same rule: any
function that needs to allocate memory accepts an allocator parameter.
As a result, the Zig Standard Library can be used even for the
freestanding target.
.PP
Zig provides \fBdefer\fR and \fBerrdefer\fR to make all resource
management, not only memory, simple and easily verifiable.
.SS Error handling
Errors are values and may not be ignored. Errors can be handled with
\fBcatch\fR. The keyword \fBtry\fR is a shortcut for
\fBcatch |err| return err\fR.
.PP
\fBunreachable\fR is used to assert that no errors will occur. This
invokes undefined behavior in the unsafe build modes, so use it only
when success is guaranteed.
.SS Stack traces on all targets
Stack traces and error return traces work on all Tier 1 and some Tier 2
targets, including freestanding. The standard library can capture a
stack trace at any point and dump it to standard error later. The
standard library's DebugAllocator uses this technique to report leaks
and double frees.
.SS Generic data structures and functions
Types are values that must be known at compile-time. A generic data
structure is a function that returns a type.
.SS Compile-time reflection and code execution
The \fB@typeInfo\fR builtin function provides reflection. The Zig
Standard Library uses this technique to implement formatted printing
entirely in Zig. In C and Rust, formatted printing is hard-coded into
the compiler.
.PP
Zig can evaluate functions and blocks of code at compile-time. In some
contexts, such as global variable initializations, the expression is
implicitly evaluated at compile-time. Otherwise, one can explicitly
evaluate code at compile-time with the \fBcomptime\fR keyword.
.SS Integration with C libraries without FFI/bindings
\fB@cImport\fR directly imports types, variables, functions, and simple
macros for use in Zig. It even translates inline functions from C into
Zig. This is accomplished by directly importing the C header file, with
no API bindings.
.SS Zig is also a C compiler
Zig can compile C code directly:
.nf
    zig build-exe hello.c -lc
    ./hello
    Hello world
.fi
.PP
Use \fB--verbose-cc\fR to see the C compiler command that was executed.
This is thanks to Build Artifact Caching. Zig automatically parses the
\fB.d\fR file using a robust caching system to avoid duplicating work.
.SS Export functions, variables, and types for C code
The \fBexport\fR keyword in front of functions, variables, and types
causes them to be part of the library API. To make a static library:
.nf
    zig build-lib mathtest.zig
.fi
.PP
To make a shared library:
.nf
    zig build-lib mathtest.zig -dynamic
.fi
.SS Cross-compiling is a first-class use case
Zig builds for all supported targets independently of the host. There is
no separate cross toolchain. To build a Hello World for
\fBx86_64-windows\fR, \fBx86_64-macos\fR, and \fBaarch64-linux\fR:
.nf
    zig build-exe hello.zig -target x86_64-windows
    zig build-exe hello.zig -target x86_64-macos
    zig build-exe hello.zig -target aarch64-linux
.fi
.PP
This works on any Tier 3 or higher target, for any Tier 3 or higher
target.
.SS Zig ships with libc
Zig ships with libc for many targets. This means \fB-lc\fR for these
targets does not depend on any system files. The total size of libc
headers that Zig ships is 130 MiB uncompressed, but Zig binary tarballs
remain roughly 50 MiB total, despite supporting libc for all these
targets, as well as compiler-rt, libunwind, libcxx, and libtsan.
.SH BUILD SYSTEM AND PACKAGE MANAGER
Zig comes with a build system, so a separate tool to orchestrate the
build process is not needed:
.nf
    zig init
    info: created build.zig
    info: created build.zig.zon
    info: created src/main.zig
    info: created src/root.zig
.fi
.PP
The build system provides steps including \fBinstall\fR, \fBuninstall\fR,
\fBrun\fR, and \fBtest\fR. Project-specific options include
\fB-Dtarget\fR, \fB-Dcpu\fR, \fB-Dofmt\fR, \fB-Ddynamic-linker\fR, and
\fB-Doptimize\fR with supported values \fBDebug\fR, \fBReleaseSafe\fR,
\fBReleaseFast\fR, and \fBReleaseSmall\fR.
.PP
System integration options include \fB--search-prefix\fR,
\fB--sysroot\fR, \fB--libc\fR, \fB--system\fR, \fB-fsys\fR, and
\fB-fno-sys\fR.
.PP
General options include \fB-p\fR/\fB--prefix\fR (default:
\fBzig-out\fR), \fB--prefix-lib-dir\fR, \fB--prefix-exe-dir\fR,
\fB--prefix-include-dir\fR, and \fB--release\fR.
.PP
Run the build:
.nf
    zig build run
    All your codebase are belong to us.
    Run `zig build test` to run the tests.
.fi
.SH TARGET SUPPORT
Zig uses a support tier system to communicate the level of support for
different targets.
.PP
Notable changes in 0.17.0:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaarch64-openbsd\fR is now tested natively in Zig's CI.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaarch64-freebsd\fR and \fBaarch64-netbsd\fR CI jobs now run on pull
requests too.
.IP \(bu 2
An LLVM bug that broke most \fBaarch64-windows\fR binaries has been
worked around.
.IP \(bu 2
Zig now provides stack traces on crashes and failed assertions on
32-bit ARM and SPARC.
.IP \(bu 2
Support for \fBloongarch32-linux-gnu[sf]\fR targets has been added.
.IP \(bu 2
Zig now has generally usable support for 64-bit SPARC, especially
\fBsparc64-linux\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
The Standard Library has been ported to the x32 and N32 ABIs on x86-64
and 64-bit MIPS, respectively.
.IP \(bu 2
Target information has been added for some game consoles:
\fBaarch64-switch\fR, \fBarm-gba\fR, \fBmipsel-psx\fR, and
\fBpowerpc-wiiu\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Very early \fBxtensa-linux\fR support has been added.
.SH DOCUMENTATION
The Zig language reference is all on one page so it can be searched with
the browser's search tool. The code samples in the official
documentation are compiled and tested as part of the main test suite of
Zig. The HTML version depends on no external files, so it can be used
offline.
.PP
Additional resources:
.IP \(bu 2
In-depth Overview: a feature overview from a systems-programming
perspective.
.IP \(bu 2
Why Zig When There is Already C++, D, and Rust?: an introduction for
programmers coming from those languages.
.IP \(bu 2
Code Examples: a list of snippets to get a feeling for how Zig code
looks.
.IP \(bu 2
Zig Build System: introduction to the Zig build system.
.SH VERSION NOTES
Zig 0.17.0 features 5 months of work: changes from 206 contributors,
spread among 925 commits.
.PP
Major changes:
.IP \(bu 2
The Build System was reworked, including the introduction of the Build
Server Protocol.
.IP \(bu 2
The ELF Linker was enhanced to the point where Incremental Compilation
is expected to work for everyone on \fBx86_64-linux\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@bitCast\fR has been redesigned.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@intFromEnum\fR and \fB@enumFromInt\fR are replaced by
\fB@backingInt\fR and \fB@fromBackingInt\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Array multiplication syntax is removed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@cImport\fR has been removed entirely.
.IP \(bu 2
The formal grammar (grammar.peg) and the handwritten parser now agree.
